<wpf:ResourceDictionary xml:space="preserve"
                        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                        xmlns:s="clr-namespace:System;assembly=mscorlib"
                        xmlns:ss="urn:shemas-jetbrains-com:settings-storage-xaml"
                        xmlns:wpf="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation">

  <!--
    Project-scoped ReSharper severities for the Qodana Community for .NET scan. See
    TotallyHotArcRouter.Qodana.slnx.DotSettings for why these decisions live in .DotSettings files
    rather than in qodana.yaml.

    Short-lived 'HttpClient': the inspection guards against socket exhaustion from churning real
    HttpClients in production. Every one of its 37 findings is in this project, where the client is
    bound to an in-memory TestServer handler and deliberately scoped to a single test so cases cannot
    leak state into each other - there is no socket to exhaust, and sharing one client across tests
    would trade a non-problem for real cross-test coupling.

    Scoped to this project on purpose rather than turned off solution-wide, so a short-lived
    HttpClient in production code is still reported.
  -->
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ShortLivedHttpClient/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

  <!--
    "Fold this into an object or collection initializer."

    All 63 are arrange blocks in this project, and the rewrite makes them worse. A test that reads

        var ledger = new StubLedger();
        ledger.MaxTurnBySession["sess-1"] = 4;   // simulates a restart mid-conversation

    becomes a nested-property initializer with nowhere to put that trailing comment, and the setup
    stops reading as a sequence of arranged facts. Several others would collapse four separate
    DefaultHttpContext.Request assignments into one nested initializer, which is harder to scan and
    harder to diff when a single field changes.

    Scoped to this project rather than turned off solution-wide, so the same finding in production
    code is still reported.
  -->
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=UseObjectOrCollectionInitializer/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

  <!--
    "Merge these checks into one pattern."

    The seven production sites were read individually and four were applied - the genuinely better
    ones, where `is JsonArray { Count: > 0 }` and `is > 0 and <= Ceiling` say what the conjunction
    was spelling out. The seventeen here are assertions and arrange conditions, where merging buys
    nothing: a test's `&&` chain is usually enumerating the conditions it means to check, and a
    merged pattern hides that enumeration.

    This is also what ADR-0008 asks for directly: score smells on production code, because "test
    projects are the regression net, not the catalog". Production stays enabled.
  -->
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=MergeIntoPattern/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

  <!--
    "This return type can be made non-nullable."

    Six of the seven are test doubles implementing a production interface whose own signature is
    nullable - IRegretBaselineRouter.Route returns null to mean "this router abstains on this task",
    IModelPriceLookup returns null for an unpriced model, IStaticAnalyzer returns null for clean
    code. A stub that happens never to return null in one test is not evidence about the contract,
    and narrowing the stub makes it stop mirroring the interface it exists to stand in for.

    The seventh was production (AnthropicStreamTranslator.HandleMessageStop) and has been applied.
  -->
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ReturnTypeCanBeNotNullable/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

  <!--
    "This null check is always false / this ?. qualifier is never null."

    Both pairs are defensive checks the nullable annotations declare unnecessary, at boundaries where
    the annotation is a claim rather than a guarantee: InMemoryProviderConfigStore guards an options
    object filled by the configuration binder, which writes nulls through non-nullable properties by
    reflection, and the Moq sites index into an object[] of captured invocation arguments. Deleting
    a guard because an annotation promises it cannot fire is how the annotation's being wrong turns
    into a NullReferenceException.
  -->
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ConditionIsAlwaysTrueOrFalseAccordingToNullableAPIContract/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ConditionalAccessQualifierIsNonNullableAccordingToAPIContract/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

  <!--
    "This method has an async overload."

    Both remaining sites are SqliteCommand.ExecuteReader, and taking the advice would be a pure loss.
    Microsoft.Data.Sqlite's async methods are not asynchronous: SQLite exposes no async I/O, so
    ExecuteReaderAsync and ReadAsync call straight through to their synchronous counterparts and
    hand back an already-completed Task. Converting buys no thread release and costs a state machine
    and a Task allocation per call, on a path a test runs in a loop.

    Genuine async overloads elsewhere - File.WriteAllTextAsync, StreamReader.ReadToEndAsync,
    CancellationTokenSource.CancelAsync - were applied rather than suppressed.
  -->
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=MethodHasAsyncOverload/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

  <!--
    "This parameter is only used for a precondition check."

    All five are lambda parameters inside Assert.All or a handler stub, used only in the assertion
    that is the point of the test - `Assert.All(models, m => Assert.NotNull(m.Id))` uses `m` for
    nothing else by design. The inspection is describing what a test assertion looks like.
  -->
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ParameterOnlyUsedForPreconditionCheck_002ELocal/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

  <!--
    "This collection is only updated, never queried" / "this member can be private."

    Both pairs are JudgeShadowScoreDrainServiceTests' fake store recording MarkCompleted and
    MarkAbandoned into Completed and Abandoned. The inspection is correct that no test reads them,
    and the honest fix is a test that asserts the drain service marks each job - not deleting the
    recording or hiding it behind `private`, either of which would remove the affordance a future
    test needs. Left public and noted; see the PR discussion.
  -->
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=CollectionNeverQueried_002ELocal/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=MemberCanBePrivate_002ELocal/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

</wpf:ResourceDictionary>
